# Two-shot zensim targeting: how close can EXACTLY TWO encodes land?
#
# The objective here is NOT "what fraction converge inside a tolerance
# band in N encodes" -- that was the previous question and it turned out to
# be the wrong one, for a reason recorded below. It is:
#
#     given exactly two encodes, minimise |achieved - target|
#
# Tolerance is a reporting parameter in these files, never a stopping rule:
# every arm spends its two encodes regardless.
#
# date          2026-08-06
# host          Darwin 26.5.2 (macOS), Apple aarch64, 12 cores, 24 GiB RAM
# rustc         rustc 1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14)
#
# ENCODER PROVENANCE — pinned, because the encoder moved twice during this
# work and an unlabelled number is worthless
#   ravif       619d81adcaa5dd5546d55bf669560e9eeb74d080 ("zenrav1e 0.1.4 ->
#               sibling master; per-SB quantizer hints are LIVE"), extracted
#               READ-ONLY with `git archive` into a sibling directory and
#               depended on by path. The ravif repo's own working tree and
#               index were never touched (verified `git status --porcelain`
#               empty against it before and after), because a concurrent
#               agent was editing it.
#               ravif/src content sha256
#               361e222c74eb655a6bfb2d907e46222fab80c3947874b73a75b7203d452926b1
#   zenrav1e    4d1ed620703d7eb94bb24467b44c5b498896af62, clean
#               src content sha256
#               aa81fbf7210246d162d3b8952ba93393cba07dbef9448f6c7527ff3edc488bc7
#   zenavif     97871e47 + the two-shot commits on top
#   binary      sha256 b030f1ba07cd8a4f3a0b804d55452aa80bebfa678aa0cb9c2f660667f47a5687
#   zensim      git imazen/zensim main @ e4b875b5 (0.3.0),
#               ZensimProfile::codec_target() == B
#   FRAME_HINTS_LIVE = true
#   NOT built with -C target-cpu=native
#
#   Build-once discipline: every cell of every arm ran on that one
#   statically linked binary, so edits landing in sibling crates mid-run
#   could not move the target.
#
#   WHAT THIS STATE IS NOT: ravif's five release-gated speed-tier arms
#   (S1_DEEP, S6_TX_SIZE_RDO, S6_PART_PRUNE, S6_INTRA7, S10_RETIER) are all
#   OFF at 619d81a. Four of them were armed in c69050a shortly after this
#   run started. These numbers therefore describe the pre-arm encoder.
#
# IS THAT STALE? Measured rather than assumed, with
# scripts/hyperparam/compare_lattice_encoders.py against the pre-bump
# (zenrav1e 0.1.4) anchor sweep, joined on identical (image, size,
# QUANTIZER) triples:
#   168 comparable cells: 72 byte-identical; zensim delta median +0.0000,
#   |d| median 0.073, |d| p90 0.99, max 2.80; bytes |d| p90 0.311%.
#   On the narrower slice overlapping benchmarks/zensim_score_lattice_
#   2026-08-06.tsv (2 sources @256, quantizers 71..150): 31/31 identical in
#   BOTH bytes and score.
# SCOPE: that is "no material change AT THESE CONFIGS ON THESE CELLS". It
# does NOT license "the bump was small" generally — encoder changes are
# speed-preset and content dependent, and the deep tiers the arms turn on
# are exactly where the partition/split-cost work bites.
#
# command      scripts/hyperparam/run_zensim_two_shot.sh <outdir>
#              (build once + provenance + lattice + fit + ab2 + summary)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FILES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# zensim_two_shot_lattice_{train,val}_2026-08-06.tsv.zst
#     Dense ACHIEVABLE-SCORE lattice: the measured score at EVERY reachable
#     AV1 quantizer index in the band covering zensim 12..96, per cell.
#     Columns: image size w h q qindex bytes zensim dm_mean enc_ms
#
#     WHY PER QUANTIZER AND NOT PER QUALITY. Encoded output depends on
#     `quality` only through the integer quantizer it resolves to, and all
#     256 quantizers are addressable (zenavif::quality_for_quantizer, which
#     round-trips for every one of them). Integer qualities reach only 100
#     of the 256 — over quality 50..80 there are 80 achievable encodes, not
#     31. The earlier conclusion that "a +-0.5 band contains no achievable
#     score about half the time" came from an integer-quality sweep and is
#     therefore ~2.56x too pessimistic: it measured the sweep grid, not the
#     codec.
#
#     Because this table is the COMPLETE outcome set for a cell, offline
#     replay of a placement rule against it is EXACT — every pass-1 and
#     pass-2 encode a rule asks for is looked up, never modelled.
#
# zensim_two_shot_fit_2026-08-06.txt
#     Lattice geometry, rule comparison on TRAIN and on held-out VAL, paired
#     rule tests with a two-sided sign test, the overshoot-policy table.
#
# zensim_two_shot_ab2_2026-08-06.tsv.zst + .summary.txt
#     REAL encodes, six arms, every one capped at two encodes, on the
#     held-out VAL sources only:
#       twoshot           the shipped rule (quantizer-space translate,
#                         nearest lattice point)
#       twoshot_atleast   same, biased to not undershoot
#       twoshot_spatial   same + the now-live per-SB map at strength 0.5
#       loop2             the existing closed loop capped at 2
#       loop2_nospatial   the same with the map suppressed
#       secant2           the baseline
#
# corpus       TRAIN benchmarks/zensim_loop_manifest_2026-08-06.txt
#              VAL   benchmarks/zensim_lattice_manifest_val_2026-08-06.txt
#              Disjoint by SOURCE, not by cell — the same photo at two sizes
#              is not two independent samples. TRAIN is the corpus the
#              existing anchor constants were fitted on, so it cannot be
#              used to judge them.
#
# sizes        long edges 64 (tiny) / 256 / 1024 for the lattice; 64 / 256
#              for ab2. There is no 4096 photographic tier: clic2025 tops
#              out at a 2048 long edge and upscaling is banned, so a 4096
#              cell would be a synthetic upscale. The only in-corpus
#              >=4096 sources are tall web screenshots (1313 x N) — a
#              corpus limit, stated rather than papered over.
#
# targets      zensim 20..90 step 5, i.e. the LOW range sampled at the same
#              density as the high one. A grid that thins out below 60
#              hides the regime where the score-vs-quantizer curve is
#              flattest and placement is hardest.
#
# scheduling   nice -n 5, NOT nice -n 19: macOS maps high nice values onto
#              the background QoS class (efficiency cores only), ~40x
#              wall-clock. The box was shared with a concurrent heavy job
#              throughout, so enc_ms and the ab2 `ms` column are NOT
#              comparable across rows and should be used for nothing.
#
# STORAGE      TSVs over 30 KB are committed zstd-compressed per the repo
#              rule. Read with:  zstd -dc <file>.tsv.zst | less
#              The .txt/.summary.txt files carry the aggregates already.
